ee that the  inside of the skull is actually a little  bit  lighter and a little bit warmer of a  color than what's on the surface  this is also visible from the parts of  the skull that are broken like the  broken horn sitting in the mud this  material is completely unique to the  giant skull and does not appear  anywhere else in the game so the  immediate question we have to ask  is is this giant skull made of rock or  is it actual bone  now there's actually a very simple way  to answer this question that  previously i didn't really think about  doing but have since been  enlightened if we comb through the  game's object file names  like i did with this map by mr cheese  that took forever because there's no  area search option meaning i had to  guess names one by one until coming  across the right one we can actually see  the name of the giant skull  object it's listed as fld obj  underscore enemy base rock underscore a  underscore zero  one clearly the developers of breath of  the wild knew that this was a  rock so we already have an easy answer  for that question that  immediately removes any speculations  about monsters from times past that the  skull could have come from  admittedly it's a little disappointing  however we still have plenty of  questions that need answers even if the  giant skull isn't an  actual skull if this giant skull is in  fact made of rock  then what rock was it made from as i  detailed more in a previous video about  the breach of demise  i personally believe that this jutting  yellowish rock that only appears in a  few places of the game  is a kind of limestone or other  sedimentary rock  that oftentimes formulates underneath  the surface of the ground  for these rock shards to be exposed like  this and the giant skull to be located  in the middle of them i think the answer  is staring us in the face  it was carved from this limestone-ish  rock right here  giving a reason for the lighter color on  the inside as for who carved the skull  out of the rock i think that answer is  also pretty obvious  i mean it is filled with members of  ganon's army so probably them  mccobbins are also well known for having  carved out the bakoblin camp skulls  which is what i believe this low-level  loner down here was doing before you  know being killed by link  the pool of mud that formed around the  giant skull probably did so  because of the crater-like shape left in  the ground as a result of all the  carving  in fact if we test the depth of this  mudpool in-game using magnesis to lower  a metallic object into it we can  actually see that it's not bottomless  at all it's maybe 15 to 20 feet deep at  the most  and the result of the shallow dirt and  debris filled rainwater basin  is a bunch of mud that's just too thick  to swim in and because  everything in this area that we can see  today would have been essentially  created by ganon's monsters that would  explain why there's no shrine in the  area as the entire hillside that we see  today  didn't really exist like this back when  the sheikah created all that shika stuff  now onto the biggest question of this  entire theory  why why did these blinds randomly decide  to make a big  scary giant monster skull when their  other smaller scary giant skulls have  worked just fine for the rest of them  across hyrule  the answer i believe lies in a nearby  goopy substance  and i don't mean the mud you see  strangely as i pointed out earlier  ganon's malice is abundant in this area  right beside the mud pool and on a lot  of the limestone actually  most fans are very familiar with the  substance by now and one thing that most  people agree on regarding malice is the  fact that it is most likely  sentient or an extension of ganon's will  this is proven by several things such as  the fact that we can see eyes in the  mellis that can search out an attack  link or the fact that it uses its more  solidified portions to block out  important passageways from the hero that  it must have deduced were important  and of course there is the fact that it  can possess and control the sheikah  technologies and inanimate skeletons of  deceased enemies  because of this we can assume that  malice is where malice wants to be  where it chooses to be and it's choosing  to be right here  right beside this random giant monster  rock carving of a skull  and after thinking through countless  possibilities i would like to present to  you the most  possible explanation in my opinion what  if the blinds were commanded by ganon to  construct a  giant stall monster for ganon to possess  using his malice according to creating a  champion the form of calamity ganon we  fight at the end of the game had only  begun to be created after link woke up  in the present day  so are we really supposed to believe  that ganon just sat still with zelda for  the past 100 years without a single  backup plan  all the way until link resurrected i  mean technically his  ultimate plan to unleash the power of  the shika to take over the world kind of  failed 100 years ago when zelda  discovered she could just poof them away  at will  so what if ganon concocted a different  plan  one that enabled him to complete his  destruction of hyrule as a giant  monster while zelda was busy keeping his  main consciousness sealed in the castle  i don't think the fact that the giant  skull is made of rock is much of a  problem for this theory  we can clearly see at other points in  the franchise that inanimate objects are  more than capable of being possessed so  i am completely positive that ganon has  the power to possess an inanimate object  such as a carved rock monster fossil if  he wanted to  one last thing to point out about this  theory though would be something  specific that zelda says in the english  version of the game that many fans have  taken  a huge issue with after calamity ganon  is defeated at the hands of link  he re-materializes as dark beast ganon  out in hyrule field  while 